    "lyova": "Лёва"
  },
  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "After the evening shift, Fedya and Senya nudge Lyova to finally talk to his crush, the cleaner Gulya. When Lyova steps into the dark kitchen, he spots Ainura and Gulya packing leftover pastries.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "gulya",
      "t": "Айнура, так делать совсем нельзя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "gulya",
      "t": "Ведь это настоящее воровство.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что ты такое говоришь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вот у Сени — это воровство.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пойми, эти пирожки утром испортятся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Их просто в мусорку выкинут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня каждый раз сердце болит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Попробуй сама, как это вкусно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пару штучек возьмём на юбилей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "gulya",
      "t": "Мне кажется, там кто-то есть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мне тоже казалось, что шеф наблюдает.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Она сейчас там совсем одна.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "gulya",
      "t": "Айнура, так делать совсем нельзя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Ainura, we really should not do this.' Gulya anchors the line on 'Ainura', while 'one must not' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "gulya",
      "t": "Ведь это настоящее воровство.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'After all, this is real theft.' The grammar packs 'after all' and 'stealing' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что ты такое говоришь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What are you talking about?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'come on' and finishing on 'speak'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вот у Сени — это воровство.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Now what Senya does—that is theft.' In Russian, the idea of 'here' flows naturally into 'stealing' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пойми, эти пирожки утром испортятся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Understand, these pastries will spoil by morning.' In Russian, the idea of 'understand' flows naturally into 'to spoil' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Их просто в мусорку выкинут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They will simply throw them in the trash.' Notice how Russian expresses 'their' and 'to throw out' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня каждый раз сердце болит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My heart aches every single time.' Ainura anchors the line on 'at', while 'hurts' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Попробуй сама, как это вкусно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Taste for yourself how delicious it is.' Ainura brings out the contrast between 'taste' and 'myself' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пару штучек возьмём на юбилей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We will take a couple of pieces for the anniversary.' Ainura anchors the line on 'couple', while 'anniversary' completes the thought. Gulya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "gulya",
      "t": "Мне кажется, там кто-то есть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It seems to me someone is over there.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'to me' before landing on 'there is'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мне тоже казалось, что шеф наблюдает.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It seemed to me too that the chef was watching.' The meaning hinges on 'to me' working together with 'to observe' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Она сейчас там совсем одна.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She is over there all alone right now.' In Russian, the idea of 'she' flows naturally into 'one' without extra filler words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
